endencies; we can propose a candidate that uses #pragma omp simd for the distance calculation across all points, then performs top-k selection using a max-heap or partial sort? However changing algorithm might affect tie-breaking.\n\nWe need preserve exact output. The original insertion maintains sorted order of distances ascending, with ties: if d < distances[x] (strictly less) OR distances[x]==-1, it inserts at first position where either condition holds. If equal distance to existing, it does not insert because d < distances[x] is false and distances[x] != -1; so it skips that point? Actually loop x from 0..k-1: if (d < distances[x] || distances[x]==-1) then insert at x and break. If d equals some existing distance, condition false for all x until maybe a -1 slot; but since array is sorted ascending with no gaps except trailing -1s, equal values will not be inserted because they are not less than any existing value and there may still be -1 slots later? Wait if k=5 and distances = [1.0, 2.0, -1,-1,-1], d=2.0: x=0 false (d<1 no), x=1 false (d<2 no), x=2 true because distances[2]==-1, so insert at index 2 shifting? Actually it will shift from y=k-1 down to x+1, moving -1s and labels; then set distances[2]=2.0. So equal values are inserted after existing equals (since they go into first -1 slot). This means the top-k list includes later points with same distance if there is room. That's important for tie-breaking: among k nearest, if multiple points have same distance at boundary, which ones are included? The algorithm processes points in input order; it inserts a point if its distance is less than some existing or there is an empty slot. So the first k distinct distances? Actually it maintains sorted list of smallest distances seen so far, with duplicates allowed up to k slots. When full (no -1), only strictly smaller distances replace larger ones. So at end, we have k points with smallest distances; ties are broken by input order: earlier points with same distance