stead of nested loops over groups and points, we can iterate over each point in each group, compute distance into a local variable d (as before), but to enable SIMD we need to process multiple points at once. We could restructure: For each group g:\n  - Allocate float *dists = malloc(sizeof(float)*g.length);\n  - Use #pragma omp simd aligned(dists:64) for j in [0,g.length): dists[j] = euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(to_evaluate, g.points[j]);\n  - Then for each j, do the insertion using d=dists[j].\nThis allows SIMD on distance computation. But allocation per group may be costly; we can reuse a buffer of max length across groups by first finding max length? Or allocate once with size equal to total points? Simpler: use VLA float dists[g.length]; but g.length could be large (maybe up to millions) causing stack overflow. Better malloc.\n\nWe also need alignment for SIMD; we can use posix_memalign or aligned_alloc. But the compiler may still vectorize without explicit alignment if it knows pointer is aligned? We can add __builtin_assume_aligned or just rely on -O3 auto-vectorization with unaligned loads.\n\nAlternatively, we could compute distances into a preallocated array of size total_points after parsing all groups. That requires two passes: first count total points, allocate arrays xs, ys, labels (char), then fill them while iterating groups again? But we already have groups; we can just iterate and store each point's x,y,label in flat arrays as we parse? We could modify main to build flat arrays during parsing. That might be more invasive.\n\nSimpler: In knn(), for each group, allocate dists array of length g.length using malloc, compute with OpenMP SIMD loop, then selection. This is a bounded change and likely improves distance computation speed significantly if compiler vectorizes. But previous experiments already achieved 0.03s; maybe they did exactly this plus alignment/restrict.\n\nWe can propose adding #pragma omp simd to the inner loop over points in each group