ts. The compiler may not vectorize due to structure-of-arrays? Actually reading two arrays of Point (each 8 bytes) and computing distance; SIMD can load pairs of floats if we use separate x/y arrays, but here each point is a struct with x,y contiguous, so loading two consecutive points gives four floats; could still vectorize. But better to create local variables px = g.points[j].x etc.\n\nWe can also add restrict qualifiers: `float * __restrict dists` and maybe `Point * __restrict pts = g.points;` but inside function we have Group g by value, so points pointer is already not aliased with dists. We can declare local pointers with restrict.\n\nAlso ensure no dependency between iterations: each writes to distinct dists[j], reads from g.points[j] and to_evaluate (invariant). So SIMD legal.\n\nAfter computing distances, run the original insertion loop using d = dists[j].\n\nFree dists after group.\n\nThis should preserve exact semantics because distance values are computed identically (same float operations) in same order? The vectorized computation may reorder floating-point additions/multiplications if compiler uses FMA or reassociation. That could change results slightly due to non-associativity of FP, potentially changing tie-breaking and output for edge cases where distances compare equal within rounding error. To be safe, we should ensure the distance formula is computed exactly as original: ((b.x - a.x) * (b.x - a.x)) + ((b.y - a.y) * (b.y - a.y)). The compiler may contract to FMA if allowed; but with default -O3 on ARM64, GCC might use FMA and change rounding. To preserve exact output bytes, we need bit-identical distances? The original sequential code compiled with same flags would also potentially use FMA if auto-vectorized? But baseline likely not vectorized due to dependency; however the scalar computation may still be contracted by compiler at -O3 (GCC default -ffp-contract=fast). So both versions might produce same results. But we cannot guarantee across different optimization levels. The official comparison is against reference sequential implementation, which may have been compiled with same flags? Actually they compare output bytes to the provided sequential program's output; if our distance computation differs in last bit for some inputs, could change ordering and thus label selection. To be safe, we should avoid any FP reassociation that changes results. We can use `#pragma omp simd` but compiler may still contract. Could add `-ffp-contract=off`? Can't modify flags.\n\nAlternative: Use integer arithmetic? No, inputs are floats.\n\nMaybe previous experiments already used SIMD and passed correctness (all valid), so FP differences didn't matter for test cases or the reference also uses same contraction. Since many prior role B experiments with OpenMP SIMD were correct, it's likely safe.\n\nWe can propose this candidate: per-group distance array computed with OpenMP SIMD aligned/restrict, then sequential selection unchanged.\n\nBut we need ensure no memory leak; free dists after each group.\n\nAlso maybe use `#pragma omp simd safelen(4)`? Not needed.\n\nLet's write the modified knn.c. We'll also modify header to add restrict qualifiers to euclidean_distance_no_sqrt parameters? That might help inlining and vectorization. But function is called inside SIMD loop; if it's not inlined, SIMD won't work. It's defined in header as non-static inline? Actually it's a regular function definition in header (not static inline), so each translation unit gets its own copy? Since knn.c includes knn.h, the function is defined there and can be inlined by compiler at -O3. We can add `static inline` to euclidean_distance_no_sqrt to encourage inlining. That might help vectorization. Also we could rewrite distance computation directly inside loop to avoid function call overhead.\n\nLet's modify header: change `float euclidean_distance_no_sqrt (Point a, Point b)` to `static inline float euclidean_distance_no_sqrt(Point a, Point b)`. This is safe and may improve performance. Also add restrict? Not needed for value parameters.\n\nIn knn.c, we'll use local variables:\n\n```\nfloat dx = g.points[j].x - to_evaluate.x;\nfloat dy = g.points[j].y - to_evaluate.y;\ndists[j] = dx*dx + dy*dy;\n```\n\nThis is equivalent and may vectorize better.\n\nWe also need include <stddef.h>? posix_memalign in stdlib.h already included via knn.h. Good.\n\nLet's craft the new knn.c:\n\n```\n#include \"knn.h\"\n\nchar knn(int n_groups, Group * groups, int k, Point to_evaluate) {\n    char * labels = (char *) malloc(sizeof(char) * k);\n    float * distances = (float *) malloc(sizeof(float) * k);\n\n    int i, j, x, y;\n\n    for (i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n        labels[i] = -1;\n        distances[i] = -1;\n    }\n\n    for (i = 0; i < n_groups; i++) {\n        Group g = groups[i];\n        float *dists = NULL;\n        if (posix_memalign((void **)&dists, 64, sizeof(float) * g.length) != 0) {\n            // fallback to malloc\n            dists = (float *)malloc(sizeof(float) * g.length);\n            if (!dists) exit(1);\n        }\n        float * __restrict rdists = dists;\n        Point * __restrict rpts = g.points;\n\n        #pragma omp simd aligned(rdists:64)\n        for (j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\n            float dx = rpts[j].x - to_evaluate.x;\n            float dy = rpts[j].y - to_evaluate.y;\n            rdists[j] = dx*dx + dy*dy;\n        }\n\n        for (j = 0; j < g.length; j++) {\n            float d = rdists[j];\n\n            for (x = 0; x < k; x++) {\n                if (d < distances[x] || distances[x] == -1) {\n                    for (y = k - 1; y > x; y--) {\n                        distances[y] = distances[y - 1];\n                        labels[y] = labels[y - 1];\n                    }\n\n                    distances[x] = d;\n                    labels[x] = g.label;\n\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        free(dists);\n    }\n\n    qsort(labels, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort);\n\n    char most_frequent = labels[0];\n    int most_frequent_count = 1;\n    int current_frequency = 1;\n\n    for (i = 1; i < k; i++) {\n        if (labels[i] != labels[i - 1]) {\n            if (current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n                most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n                most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n            }\n\n            current_frequency = 1;\n        } else {\n            current_frequency++;\n        }\n\n        if (i == k - 1 && current_frequency > most_frequent_count) {\n            most_frequent = labels[i - 1];\n            most_frequent_count = current_frequency;\n        }\n    }\n\n\n    return most_frequent;\n}\n\nint main() { ... unchanged ... }\n```\n\nBut note: `#pragma omp simd aligned(rdists:64)` requires rdists to be actually 64-byte aligned. posix_memalign ensures that if it succeeds; but fallback malloc may not align. We could avoid fallback and just use malloc, then no aligned clause or use unaligned SIMD. Better to always use posix_memalign and handle failure by exit? But we shouldn't change behavior on OOM; original would also fail. We can simply call posix_memalign and if fails, free all and return? Simpler: use `float *dists = aligned_alloc(64, sizeof(float)*g.length);` but C11 aligned_alloc may not be available with older GCC? It is in stdlib.h for C11. But to be safe, we can just use malloc without alignment; the compiler can still vectorize unaligned loads on ARM64 (NEON supports unaligned). The `aligned` clause is a hint; if pointer not aligned, it's UB? Actually OpenMP spec says behavior undefined if data isn't aligned as specified. So better not specify alignment unless guaranteed.\n\nWe could use `#pragma omp simd` without aligned. That should still vectorize with unaligned loads. Previous experiments used aligned allocations and got good results; but we can propose using posix_memalign to guarantee 64-byte alignment, then add aligned clause. We'll handl